[05:00] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The KISS-GS paper webpage is shown, displaying a 3D scene viewer and text about "3D Gaussian Splatting Compression Kept Simple".
AUDIO: 'is so exciting is this is actually the original team from SOG. Not PlayCanvas SOG, not spatially oriented Gaussians, but Self-Organizing Gaussians. KISS breaks the compression pipeline down into three simple steps. The first is'

[05:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'PopSplat, which prunes away unused Gaussians. Then, they have something called SOG XT, which then sorts the remaining Gaussians. And then finally, they have something called PRESS, which stands for Parallel Representative Assignment Smoothing. And that basically'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: The presenter discusses job opportunities in Gaussian Splatting.
AUDIO: 'helps guide the final quantization. And you\'re probably super curious about the actual final compression. So, on the garden scene, which we all of course know and love, they were able to compress that about 230 times down to about 3 and 1/2 MB, which is really'

[05:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ridiculous. So, file sizes for Gaussian splats are going to get smaller yet again. The repo has not yet been published with the code, but I have also been speaking to the paper authors as well quite a bit over the last week, and'

